{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize small-scale drug possession is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reinstates penalties for holding substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals found with these drugs can either face a misdemeanor charge—potentially leading to six months in jail—or opt into a health-focused intervention. This includes enrollment in a behavioral program and engagement with a diversion initiative designed to steer people away from fines and incarceration.\n\n***\n\nThe updated law empowers law enforcement to act more decisively in public spaces, particularly near areas like playgrounds and recreational zones, where drug sales will now carry stiffer consequences. While counties are encouraged to set up support pathways tied to mental health and addiction care, they are not required to do so. This flexibility has sparked debate: supporters argue it allows local adaptation, while critics warn of inconsistent access and uneven outcomes across regions.\n\n***\n\nPolice are given strict guidelines for offering alternatives to arrest. To qualify, a person must have no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, no other charges, and must be medically stable. If all conditions are met, officers contact a regional dispatch line, triggering a mobile health team to respond within half an hour. If the team doesn’t arrive in time, arrest becomes the default option—no extended waiting is permitted.\n\n***\n\nThe system reflects a broader national conversation about how to respond to substance use. While some look to the country’s most progressive models, others question whether enforcement without guaranteed care truly serves those in need. Imagine a teenager at age 16 who eats a snack after school, goes shopping with friends, and later finds a pill on the sidewalk—what happens next? The tongue of policy speaks in conditions, not compassion.\n\nP.P.S This policy affects people of every age, in a country where shopping and eating are everyday acts, yet the tongue of justice often lingers too long on punishment instead of care.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.9086962342262268}, {"token": "-scale", "logprob": -0.040357448160648346}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.0009162995265796781}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.002277996391057968}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.010889627039432526}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.17439132928848267}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.283170461654663}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.040426600724458694}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.3591260313987732}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.05537395551800728}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -1.2040065485052764e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.01690572127699852}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.14491938054561615}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.26086822152137756}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.0002451834443490952}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -1.2039388418197632}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -1.1234886646270752}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -2.4676019165781327e-05}, {"token": " holding", "logprob": -0.512071967124939}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.051119364798069}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.00018010901112575084}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.002